WEBVTT

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.170
The stereotype of someone
with social anxiety as a recluse

00:04:33.203 --> 00:04:35.806
who'd rather be hiding away
alone in their bedroom

00:04:35.806 --> 00:04:37.541
than out interacting with the world.

00:04:37.574 --> 00:04:39.009
And this is simply not true.

00:04:39.409 --> 00:04:41.678
In research conducted in my lab,

00:04:41.711 --> 00:04:43.980
we find that people
with social anxiety disorder

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.250
desire strong, healthy,
intimate relationships

00:04:47.284 --> 00:04:49.920
to the same degree
as those without mental illness.

00:04:49.953 --> 00:04:51.555
And they do socialize,

00:04:51.588 --> 00:04:53.423
they’re not robotic aliens.

00:04:53.456 --> 00:04:54.724
And when they socialize,

00:04:54.758 --> 00:04:56.426
they often enjoy doing so.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.629
In fact, when we ask people
with social anxiety

00:04:59.663 --> 00:05:01.531
how happy they are,